1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,224
A reviewer approved a pull request at
eleven. The author pushed one more commit at

2
00:00:09,264 --> 00:00:12,244
ten past. The pull request
merged at a quarter past.

3
00:00:12,284 --> 00:00:15,323
The commit that reached main
last was read by nobody.

4
00:00:15,663 --> 00:00:18,750
Your CTO asks: we require
a review on every change.

5
00:00:18,790 --> 00:00:23,165
How did unreviewed code get in, and
which setting would have stopped it?

6
00:00:23,505 --> 00:00:28,279
GitHub's documentation has a word for this:
a pull request being "hijacked, where

7
00:00:28,319 --> 00:00:31,547
unapproved content is added
to approved pull requests".

8
00:00:31,587 --> 00:00:36,538
Two settings address it. They answer different
attacks, and each has a cost that you

9
00:00:36,578 --> 00:00:40,817
should be able to state before you
propose it. Hold on to that timeline.

10
00:00:40,857 --> 00:00:42,243
It comes back as a quiz.

11
00:00:44,083 --> 00:00:47,801
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:47,841 --> 00:00:52,914
A pull request is a proposal on GitHub to
merge one branch, the head, into another,

13
00:00:52,954 --> 00:00:56,856
the base. In the last video it
was an object over three commits.

14
00:00:56,896 --> 00:00:58,458
Now it moves through time.

15
00:00:58,798 --> 00:01:03,931
We follow it from draft to merged or closed,
and at each step ask the question of

16
00:01:03,971 --> 00:01:08,210
this whole part: is this GitHub
data, or does it write to a branch?

17
00:01:08,250 --> 00:01:13,575
The answer from section 17.4 is short: each
state is GitHub data, and only the merge

18
00:01:13,615 --> 00:01:14,789
writes to a branch.

19
00:01:15,129 --> 00:01:20,046
Four sections of Chapter 17: the
lifecycle, stale approvals, checks and

20
00:01:20,086 --> 00:01:25,702
mergeability, and conflicts in a pull
request. The demonstration is the conflict,

21
00:01:25,742 --> 00:01:31,498
repaired in two ways, in labs/ch17/pr-conflict.sh.
The GitHub side is a walkthrough

22
00:01:31,538 --> 00:01:35,408
of a review, which needs a
second account or a teammate.

23
00:01:35,748 --> 00:01:41,053
Labels from the chapter's table. git merge
and git rebase are 🟡 CAUTION: they move

24
00:01:41,093 --> 00:01:46,332
or rewrite the current branch. git push
--force-with-lease is 🔴 DANGEROUS, and it

25
00:01:46,372 --> 00:01:49,656
appears in this video, so
it gets its five answers.

26
00:01:49,696 --> 00:01:55,131
gh pr update-branch and gh pr merge are 🟡:
they move the head branch or write to the

27
00:01:55,171 --> 00:01:57,673
base branch, and can dismiss approvals.

28
00:01:59,513 --> 00:02:00,926
After this video you can:

29
00:02:01,266 --> 00:02:05,064
Describe the states of a pull request
from draft to merged or closed.

30
00:02:05,404 --> 00:02:09,884
Explain what a push does to an existing
approval under each of the two settings.

31
00:02:10,224 --> 00:02:14,106
Tell checks from status checks and say
what "mergeable" takes into account.

32
00:02:14,446 --> 00:02:19,118
Resolve a pull request conflict by merging
the base in or by rebasing, and say what

33
00:02:19,158 --> 00:02:20,480
each does to the review.

34
00:02:20,820 --> 00:02:23,754
Show that both resolutions
produce the same diff.

35
00:02:25,594 --> 00:02:30,426
The lifecycle, in one sentence: a pull
request moves from draft to ready for review,

36
00:02:30,466 --> 00:02:33,616
collects reviews and checks,
and ends merged or closed.

37
00:02:33,956 --> 00:02:39,074
Draft. "Draft pull requests cannot be merged,
and code owners are not automatically

38
00:02:39,114 --> 00:02:44,170
requested to review them." Code owners are
the users or teams a repository assigns

39
00:02:44,210 --> 00:02:48,894
to paths. Marking the pull request ready
"will request reviews from any code

40
00:02:48,934 --> 00:02:53,369
owners", and you can convert a pull
request back to a draft at any time.

41
00:02:53,409 --> 00:02:57,843
Use a draft when you want CI and early
comments but not a formal review.

42
00:02:57,883 --> 00:03:03,002
An outdated-advice note: since the first
of May 2025, drafts are available in every

43
00:03:03,042 --> 00:03:05,177
repository, not only on paid plans.

44
00:03:05,517 --> 00:03:10,423
Reviews. A review has one of three outcomes.
Comment: feedback without a verdict,

45
00:03:10,463 --> 00:03:15,492
which does not block. Approve: counts toward
required approvals, if a rule requires

46
00:03:15,532 --> 00:03:19,644
any. Request changes: blocks only
if a rule requires a pull request.

47
00:03:19,684 --> 00:03:24,652
GitHub's own words on that third outcome:
it "is purely informational and will not

48
00:03:24,692 --> 00:03:29,537
prevent merging unless a ruleset or classic
branch protection rule is configured

49
00:03:29,577 --> 00:03:34,605
with the 'require a pull request' option".
Without a rule, "changes requested" is a

50
00:03:34,645 --> 00:03:35,826
request, not a lock.

51
00:03:36,166 --> 00:03:40,684
Two more documented facts: anyone with read
access can review and comment, and "pull

52
00:03:40,724 --> 00:03:43,723
request authors cannot approve
their own pull requests".

53
00:03:43,763 --> 00:03:46,436
That is why the review labs
need a second account.

54
00:03:46,776 --> 00:03:49,913
Suggestions. A reviewer can
propose replacement lines.

55
00:03:49,953 --> 00:03:54,738
When the author applies them, GitHub creates
a single commit on the pull request's

56
00:03:54,778 --> 00:03:59,387
branch, on GitHub's side. Your local
branch lacks it until you pull, and a push

57
00:03:59,427 --> 00:04:02,329
without pulling is rejected
as a non-fast-forward.

58
00:04:02,669 --> 00:04:07,709
One recent item: since the first of
September 2026, in public preview and off by

59
00:04:07,749 --> 00:04:12,663
default, Copilot code review can submit
an approving review that counts toward

60
00:04:12,703 --> 00:04:17,108
required approvals. When you design
a review policy, count the humans.

61
00:04:17,448 --> 00:04:22,341
Stale approvals. In one sentence: an
approval is attached to the diff, the set of

62
00:04:22,381 --> 00:04:27,212
changes, as it was when the reviewer
approved. Two optional settings decide what

63
00:04:27,252 --> 00:04:30,744
happens to that approval when
the diff changes afterwards.

64
00:04:31,084 --> 00:04:35,641
Setting 1: dismiss stale approvals. GitHub
records the state of the diff at the

65
00:04:35,681 --> 00:04:40,237
point of approval. If the diff changes
from this state, the approving review is

66
00:04:40,277 --> 00:04:44,892
dismissed as stale, and the pull request
cannot be merged until someone approves

67
00:04:44,932 --> 00:04:49,372
again. Listen to the causes the documentation
gives: a contributor pushes new

68
00:04:49,412 --> 00:04:54,201
changes, someone clicks Update branch, or
a related pull request is merged into the

69
00:04:54,241 --> 00:04:58,332
target branch. The diff can change
without anyone touching your branch.

70
00:04:58,672 --> 00:05:02,352
Setting 2: require approval of
the most recent reviewable push.

71
00:05:02,392 --> 00:05:06,899
It requires "an approval from someone
other than the last person to push to a

72
00:05:06,939 --> 00:05:11,565
branch". With this option stale reviews
are not dismissed, and the pull request

73
00:05:11,605 --> 00:05:16,407
remains approved as long as someone other
than the person who made the most recent

74
00:05:16,447 --> 00:05:21,367
changes approves it. GitHub presents it as
a compromise for large pull requests with

75
00:05:21,407 --> 00:05:25,915
many reviewers, and says which one is
stricter: "it is safer to dismiss stale

76
00:05:25,955 --> 00:05:26,446
reviews."

77
00:05:26,786 --> 00:05:31,568
The costs. The first costs a re-review
after every update, including an "Update

78
00:05:31,608 --> 00:05:36,512
branch". The second can leave earlier
approvals standing on a diff that has since

79
00:05:36,552 --> 00:05:37,000
changed.

80
00:05:37,340 --> 00:05:40,967
Quick quiz, back to the
hook. Neither setting is on.

81
00:05:41,007 --> 00:05:46,327
A reviewer approves, then the author
pushes again. Is the pull request still

82
00:05:46,367 --> 00:05:48,936
approved? A: yes. B: no. Your answer?

83
00:05:53,776 --> 00:05:56,892
A. The approval stands, on a
diff that has since changed.

84
00:05:56,932 --> 00:05:58,885
That's how unread code reached main.

85
00:05:59,225 --> 00:06:04,328
Checks and mergeability. In one sentence:
mergeability is two independent questions.

86
00:06:04,368 --> 00:06:07,512
Can Git merge the two tips,
which is the test merge?

87
00:06:07,552 --> 00:06:09,900
And do the repository's rules allow it?

88
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:15,303
GitHub distinguishes two kinds of status
checks. Checks carry detailed output and

89
00:06:15,343 --> 00:06:20,469
are created by GitHub Apps, including
Actions. Commit statuses are a simpler state

90
00:06:20,509 --> 00:06:25,446
set through the API by external services.
"GitHub Actions generates checks, not

91
00:06:25,486 --> 00:06:30,234
commit statuses." Both are attached to
a commit ID, not to the pull request.

92
00:06:30,274 --> 00:06:33,762
A new push creates a new head
commit with no checks yet.

93
00:06:33,802 --> 00:06:37,289
And "required checks must pass
on the latest commit SHA.

94
00:06:37,329 --> 00:06:41,006
Checks from earlier commits
don't satisfy the requirement."

95
00:06:41,346 --> 00:06:46,238
A red check blocks nothing by itself.
Only a rule that names the check does.

96
00:06:46,578 --> 00:06:50,740
For scripts: the API's mergeable
field can be true, false or null.

97
00:06:50,780 --> 00:06:54,624
Null means GitHub has started a
background job to compute it.

98
00:06:54,664 --> 00:06:58,062
A script that treats null as
"not mergeable" is wrong.

99
00:06:58,102 --> 00:06:59,208
It must ask again.

100
00:06:59,548 --> 00:07:04,722
One caveat the textbook marks unverified:
two GitHub pages disagree about how long

101
00:07:04,762 --> 00:07:09,935
checks data is retained, 400 days on one,
the Actions retention setting of 90 days

102
00:07:09,975 --> 00:07:14,259
by default on a changelog entry
effective the first of October 2026.

103
00:07:14,299 --> 00:07:19,600
The practical consequence either way: an
old pull request may need its checks re-run

104
00:07:19,640 --> 00:07:20,871
before it can merge.

105
00:07:21,211 --> 00:07:26,133
Conflicts. On screen is today's example:
two commits on the branch fix/threshold,

106
00:07:26,173 --> 00:07:31,033
one new commit on main. In one sentence:
a pull request "has conflicts" when the

107
00:07:31,073 --> 00:07:35,749
test merge of head into base cannot be
computed cleanly, and you repair it by

108
00:07:35,789 --> 00:07:40,833
changing the head branch, either by merging
the base into it or by rebasing it onto

109
00:07:40,873 --> 00:07:41,384
the base.

110
00:07:41,724 --> 00:07:46,672
It is the ordinary three-way conflict from
the merge videos, detected on the server,

111
00:07:46,712 --> 00:07:51,659
where nobody can resolve it. The resolution
has to arrive as new commits on the head

112
00:07:51,699 --> 00:07:56,646
branch. GitHub offers three routes. The web
conflict editor, which merges the entire

113
00:07:56,686 --> 00:08:01,396
base branch into the head branch and
handles only simple competing line changes.

114
00:08:01,436 --> 00:08:06,146
The command line. And, with the Copilot
cloud agent, a button that has the agent

115
00:08:06,186 --> 00:08:09,590
commit a resolution, which you
review like any resolution.

116
00:08:11,430 --> 00:08:15,962
Think of an approval as a signature on
a specific printout, not on the folder.

117
00:08:16,002 --> 00:08:20,828
If a page in the folder is replaced after
the signature, the signature still exists

118
00:08:20,868 --> 00:08:23,348
and no longer covers
what is in the folder.

119
00:08:23,688 --> 00:08:28,399
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper:
draw the hook's timeline, approve, push,

120
00:08:28,439 --> 00:08:31,748
merge, and circle what the
signature covers. I'll wait.

121
00:08:36,588 --> 00:08:41,214
Your circle should stop at eleven. The
commit pushed at ten past sits outside it.

122
00:08:41,554 --> 00:08:45,737
Setting 1 tears up the signature
whenever the printout would now differ.

123
00:08:45,777 --> 00:08:50,547
Setting 2 keeps the signatures and adds a
rule about the last page: the person who

124
00:08:50,587 --> 00:08:53,538
inserted it cannot be the
only one vouching for it.

125
00:08:53,878 --> 00:08:58,798
The model breaks in one respect: with a
real folder, pages change only when someone

126
00:08:58,838 --> 00:09:02,741
opens it. A pull request's diff
can change because the base moved.

127
00:09:02,781 --> 00:09:06,207
Nobody touched your branch,
and the printout is different.

128
00:09:06,247 --> 00:09:11,047
That is why setting 1 also dismisses after
"a related pull request is merged into

129
00:09:11,087 --> 00:09:12,182
the target branch".

130
00:09:14,022 --> 00:09:18,711
Start at the left: a draft cannot be
merged, and code owners are not requested.

131
00:09:18,751 --> 00:09:21,824
"Mark ready" moves it to
ready and requests reviews.

132
00:09:21,864 --> 00:09:24,398
A review moves it to
one of three outcomes.

133
00:09:24,738 --> 00:09:28,646
Look at the arrow going back from
the review box to "ready": a push.

134
00:09:28,686 --> 00:09:31,607
Under setting 1, that arrow
dismisses the approval.

135
00:09:31,647 --> 00:09:35,556
Under neither setting, there is no
such arrow, and that is the hook.

136
00:09:35,896 --> 00:09:40,741
At the bottom right, the merge. The label
is the point of the whole diagram: it is

137
00:09:40,781 --> 00:09:43,363
the only transition that
writes to a branch.

138
00:09:47,703 --> 00:09:51,339
Ravi's branch fix/threshold
lowers the confidence threshold.

139
00:09:51,379 --> 00:09:53,728
Asha raised it on main in the meantime.

140
00:09:54,068 --> 00:09:59,048
Two commits in the pull request. The test
merge, computed locally exactly as the

141
00:09:59,088 --> 00:10:02,750
server would: exit status 1,
and the conflicted file named.

142
00:10:02,790 --> 00:10:07,894
On GitHub this pull request would show as
conflicting, with no merge ref and no CI

143
00:10:07,934 --> 00:10:08,145
run.

144
00:10:08,485 --> 00:10:13,759
Repair 1: merge the base into the head.
This is what the web editor and the Update

145
00:10:13,799 --> 00:10:17,842
branch button do, and what gh pr
update-branch does by default.

146
00:10:17,882 --> 00:10:21,147
Predict: will the merge
stop on Ravi's machine too?

147
00:10:21,187 --> 00:10:22,184
Say it out loud.

148
00:10:27,024 --> 00:10:31,147
It stops. The conflict, now in a
place where someone can resolve it.

149
00:10:31,187 --> 00:10:33,535
After resolving, adding and committing:

150
00:10:35,875 --> 00:10:40,130
The branch had two commits. After the
merge is committed and pushed: how many

151
00:10:40,170 --> 00:10:43,755
commits does the pull request list,
and does the push need force?

152
00:10:44,095 --> 00:10:47,481
Three commits, one of them a merge
that carries the resolution.

153
00:10:47,821 --> 00:10:52,188
The push was a fast-forward, because the
new tip descends from the old one: no

154
00:10:52,228 --> 00:10:55,182
force, and nobody's clone
of the branch is disturbed.

155
00:10:55,522 --> 00:11:00,835
Repair 2, in a second copy of the same two
branches: rebase the head onto the base.

156
00:11:00,875 --> 00:11:06,251
A rebase copies each of your commits onto
the new base, and every copy has a new ID.

157
00:11:09,591 --> 00:11:13,665
The rebase stops at the first
commit with the same conflict.

158
00:11:13,705 --> 00:11:16,407
Resolve, git add, git rebase --continue.

159
00:11:18,747 --> 00:11:23,630
After the rebase: how many commits in the
pull request, are their IDs the ones the

160
00:11:23,670 --> 00:11:26,572
reviewer saw, and what
does a plain git push say?

161
00:11:26,912 --> 00:11:31,662
Two commits in a straight line,
both new: be88439 became e2639c7.

162
00:11:31,702 --> 00:11:35,125
A plain push is rejected
as a non-fast-forward.

163
00:11:35,465 --> 00:11:39,776
History was rewritten, so the
push needs --force-with-lease.

164
00:11:40,116 --> 00:11:45,019
That is the 🔴 command. The five answers.
What it changes: it replaces the remote

165
00:11:45,059 --> 00:11:48,357
branch, if the remote branch
is where you last saw it.

166
00:11:48,397 --> 00:11:52,682
What it can destroy: commits on the
server that no clone of yours has.

167
00:11:52,722 --> 00:11:56,452
And alone, the check passes
wrongly after a background fetch.

168
00:11:56,792 --> 00:12:01,192
How to preview: add --dry-run,
and after a fetch look at git log

169
00:12:01,232 --> 00:12:06,326
HEAD..origin/<branch>. How to recover:
push the old ID back from a reflog.

170
00:12:06,366 --> 00:12:11,322
When it is appropriate: for your own
pull request branch after a rebase.

171
00:12:11,362 --> 00:12:12,987
Here it is exactly that.

172
00:12:13,327 --> 00:12:17,924
Also note what you cannot see any more:
the conflict was resolved inside the first

173
00:12:17,964 --> 00:12:20,356
commit. No commit records
that it happened.

174
00:12:23,696 --> 00:12:26,706
Both repairs give the reviewer
the same three-dot diff.

175
00:12:27,046 --> 00:12:32,282
Merge base into head: the push is a
fast-forward, existing commit IDs are kept, the

176
00:12:32,322 --> 00:12:37,304
resolution is visible in the merge commit,
and the commit list grows by a merge

177
00:12:37,344 --> 00:12:42,326
commit. Rebase head onto base: the push
is forced, commit IDs are replaced, the

178
00:12:42,366 --> 00:12:47,474
resolution is folded into the rewritten
commits, and the commit list stays clean.

179
00:12:47,814 --> 00:12:52,455
Under both, stale-approval dismissal is
triggered, because the diff changed.

180
00:12:52,495 --> 00:12:57,505
And the last row settles many arguments:
after a squash merge, which replaces your

181
00:12:57,545 --> 00:13:01,570
commits by one new commit, the
final history on main is identical.

182
00:13:01,610 --> 00:13:06,496
Under the squash method the shape of the
branch disappears at merge time, so the

183
00:13:06,536 --> 00:13:07,975
cheaper merge is enough.

184
00:13:08,315 --> 00:13:12,907
Under the other two methods the shape lands
on main, which is a reason to rebase.

185
00:13:13,247 --> 00:13:17,520
Open a pull request as a draft and
read the merge box: according to the

186
00:13:17,560 --> 00:13:20,496
documentation it cannot
be merged. Mark it ready.

187
00:13:20,836 --> 00:13:23,813
From the second account,
submit an approving review.

188
00:13:23,853 --> 00:13:26,655
The author cannot approve
their own pull request.

189
00:13:26,995 --> 00:13:31,772
Now push one more commit from the author's
side and open the timeline of the pull

190
00:13:31,812 --> 00:13:35,519
request. Read the events in order:
the approval, then the push.

191
00:13:35,559 --> 00:13:39,801
Whether the approval is still counted
depends on the repository's rules.

192
00:13:39,841 --> 00:13:44,321
With "dismiss stale approvals" enabled,
the documentation says the review is

193
00:13:44,361 --> 00:13:48,008
dismissed as stale. With neither
setting, the approval stands.

194
00:13:48,048 --> 00:13:53,004
Rulesets, GitHub's named lists of rules,
are the subject of the last three videos of

195
00:13:53,044 --> 00:13:57,881
this batch. For now, read what your repository
does and say which case you are in.

196
00:13:59,721 --> 00:14:01,369
Five mistakes to watch for.

197
00:14:01,709 --> 00:14:06,112
Assuming a required review covers the
final diff. Root cause: an approval is

198
00:14:06,152 --> 00:14:10,907
attached to the diff as it was; without
one of the two settings, a later push does

199
00:14:10,947 --> 00:14:11,725
not affect it.

200
00:14:12,065 --> 00:14:16,897
Treating "changes requested" as a lock.
Root cause: it is informational unless a

201
00:14:16,937 --> 00:14:18,663
rule requires a pull request.

202
00:14:19,003 --> 00:14:23,883
Expecting green checks to carry over after
a push. Root cause: checks are attached

203
00:14:23,923 --> 00:14:27,063
to a commit ID, and a push
creates a new head commit.

204
00:14:27,403 --> 00:14:30,584
Treating a null mergeable as
"not mergeable" in a script.

205
00:14:30,624 --> 00:14:33,749
Root cause: null means the
computation is still running.

206
00:14:34,089 --> 00:14:38,688
Pushing after applying a suggestion or
pressing Update branch, and being rejected.

207
00:14:38,728 --> 00:14:43,385
Root cause: GitHub made a commit on the
branch; the local branch lacks it until you

208
00:14:43,425 --> 00:14:43,668
pull.

209
00:14:45,508 --> 00:14:50,313
Now, out of the lab. The team from the
hook enables "dismiss stale approvals" on

210
00:14:50,353 --> 00:14:55,400
main. The first week brings a complaint:
the team rebases pull request branches onto

211
00:14:55,440 --> 00:15:00,124
main before merging, and now every rebase
dismisses the approval, so reviewers

212
00:15:00,164 --> 00:15:03,455
approve twice. That is the
control working as designed.

213
00:15:03,795 --> 00:15:08,357
The textbook's advice is to name the cost
when you propose the setting, and the team

214
00:15:08,397 --> 00:15:12,520
does: for small pull requests the
re-review takes a minute and they keep it.

215
00:15:12,560 --> 00:15:17,012
For the two large, long-running pull requests
with many reviewers they discuss the

216
00:15:17,052 --> 00:15:21,394
second setting, knowing GitHub's own
verdict that dismissing is the safer of the

217
00:15:21,434 --> 00:15:21,613
two.

218
00:15:21,953 --> 00:15:26,799
A second rule from the same section goes
into their contribution guide: do not

219
00:15:26,839 --> 00:15:31,497
resolve conflicts in lock files or other
generated files by picking a side.

220
00:15:31,537 --> 00:15:35,694
Take the base version, regenerate
with the tool, commit the result.

221
00:15:37,534 --> 00:15:41,356
Your turn. Do Exercise 21.2,
"Is it still approved?", in

222
00:15:41,396 --> 00:15:46,873
exercises/m19-m25-github.md. For each
sequence of events, decide before you look

223
00:15:46,913 --> 00:15:52,666
anything up whether the approval still
counts, under setting 1, under setting 2, and

224
00:15:52,706 --> 00:15:58,390
under neither. Write the reason as "the
diff changed" or "the diff did not change",

225
00:15:58,430 --> 00:15:59,907
and "who pushed last".

226
00:16:00,247 --> 00:16:05,614
The challenge is Exercise 21.7, "Green on
the pull request, red on main", in the

227
00:16:05,654 --> 00:16:06,290
same file.

228
00:16:08,130 --> 00:16:11,172
Question 270 of the CTO question bank:

229
00:16:13,512 --> 00:16:18,579
Answer out loud. A strong answer first
explains why the sequence is possible at all,

230
00:16:18,619 --> 00:16:23,625
in terms of what an approval is attached
to. It then describes each setting by what

231
00:16:23,665 --> 00:16:28,732
it does when the diff changes, including
the case where nobody pushed, and gives the

232
00:16:28,772 --> 00:16:33,717
cost of each in a team's daily work. It
says which one GitHub calls safer and when

233
00:16:33,757 --> 00:16:35,176
the other is defensible.

234
00:16:37,016 --> 00:16:41,682
Let's land this. Two repairs, one diff
for the reviewer, and an approval that

235
00:16:41,722 --> 00:16:44,188
belongs to a diff, not to a pull request.

236
00:16:44,528 --> 00:16:46,188
You should now be able to say:

237
00:16:46,528 --> 00:16:51,405
Every state of a pull request is GitHub
data; only the merge writes to a branch.

238
00:16:51,745 --> 00:16:56,116
An approval belongs to a diff; "dismiss
stale approvals" removes it when the diff

239
00:16:56,156 --> 00:17:00,527
changes, and "approval of the most recent
reviewable push" requires someone other

240
00:17:00,567 --> 00:17:02,269
than the last pusher to approve.

241
00:17:02,609 --> 00:17:07,473
Actions produces checks; both checks and
statuses belong to commit IDs, and required

242
00:17:07,513 --> 00:17:09,692
checks must pass on the latest commit.

243
00:17:10,032 --> 00:17:13,129
Mergeable means Git can merge
the tips and the rules allow it.

244
00:17:13,469 --> 00:17:18,247
A conflict is repaired on the head branch,
by merging the base in or by rebasing;

245
00:17:18,287 --> 00:17:20,804
the reviewer sees the
same diff either way.

246
00:17:22,644 --> 00:17:27,251
Read sections 17.4 to 17.7 of Chapter 17.

247
00:17:27,591 --> 00:17:32,527
You can now say what a push does to an
approval, and repair a conflicting pull

248
00:17:32,567 --> 00:17:37,439
request two ways. Practise with Exercise
21.2. Next: why a pull request shows

249
00:17:37,479 --> 00:17:42,478
